{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-93988" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2} & 7 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/state.json" "file_sha256" "730ea7ef6a323cfca08b62e3d6e93c8cf4e473947b10aed611e83a49ac0aed93"} {"question" "q-93988" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{12}\\ne {q}_{21},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $R$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/state.json" "file_sha256" "730ea7ef6a323cfca08b62e3d6e93c8cf4e473947b10aed611e83a49ac0aed93"} {"question" "q-93988" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff3c7a7ae68967c3c6de05af768f18d52c2173193bbbccf5672ce56f85ca20e7"} {"question" "q-93988" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/state.json" "file_sha256" "730ea7ef6a323cfca08b62e3d6e93c8cf4e473947b10aed611e83a49ac0aed93"} {"question" "q-93988" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$P$ and $Q$ only"] ["text" "$P$ and $R$ only"] ["text" "$P$ only"] ["text" "$P,$ $Q,$ and $R$"] ["text" "$Q$ and $R$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/state.json" "file_sha256" "730ea7ef6a323cfca08b62e3d6e93c8cf4e473947b10aed611e83a49ac0aed93"} {"question" "q-93988" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$P$ and $R$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/state.json" "file_sha256" "730ea7ef6a323cfca08b62e3d6e93c8cf4e473947b10aed611e83a49ac0aed93"} {"question" "q-96589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{2}$ is always symmetric.\n- The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is a symmetric matrix, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/state.json" "file_sha256" "730ea7ef6a323cfca08b62e3d6e93c8cf4e473947b10aed611e83a49ac0aed93"} {"question" "q-96589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{2}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any non-singular symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{-1})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{-1} \\\\  & ={A}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/state.json" "file_sha256" "730ea7ef6a323cfca08b62e3d6e93c8cf4e473947b10aed611e83a49ac0aed93"} {"question" "q-96589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff3c7a7ae68967c3c6de05af768f18d52c2173193bbbccf5672ce56f85ca20e7"} {"question" "q-96589" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/state.json" "file_sha256" "730ea7ef6a323cfca08b62e3d6e93c8cf4e473947b10aed611e83a49ac0aed93"} {"question" "q-96589" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/state.json" "file_sha256" "730ea7ef6a323cfca08b62e3d6e93c8cf4e473947b10aed611e83a49ac0aed93"} {"question" "q-96589" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/state.json" "file_sha256" "730ea7ef6a323cfca08b62e3d6e93c8cf4e473947b10aed611e83a49ac0aed93"} {"question" "q-143680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 11 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 11 & -9 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/state.json" "file_sha256" "730ea7ef6a323cfca08b62e3d6e93c8cf4e473947b10aed611e83a49ac0aed93"} {"question" "q-143680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/state.json" "file_sha256" "730ea7ef6a323cfca08b62e3d6e93c8cf4e473947b10aed611e83a49ac0aed93"} {"question" "q-143680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff3c7a7ae68967c3c6de05af768f18d52c2173193bbbccf5672ce56f85ca20e7"} {"question" "q-143680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/state.json" "file_sha256" "730ea7ef6a323cfca08b62e3d6e93c8cf4e473947b10aed611e83a49ac0aed93"} {"question" "q-143680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$P$ and $Q$ only"] ["text" "$P$ and $R$ only"] ["text" "$Q$ and $R$ only"] ["text" "$Q$ only"] ["text" "$R$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/state.json" "file_sha256" "730ea7ef6a323cfca08b62e3d6e93c8cf4e473947b10aed611e83a49ac0aed93"} {"question" "q-143680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$P$ and $Q$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/state.json" "file_sha256" "730ea7ef6a323cfca08b62e3d6e93c8cf4e473947b10aed611e83a49ac0aed93"} {"question" "q-143687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The sum of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$  matrix, then $A-{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n- The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/state.json" "file_sha256" "730ea7ef6a323cfca08b62e3d6e93c8cf4e473947b10aed611e83a49ac0aed93"} {"question" "q-143687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, $A-{A}^{T}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{A}^{T} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/state.json" "file_sha256" "730ea7ef6a323cfca08b62e3d6e93c8cf4e473947b10aed611e83a49ac0aed93"} {"question" "q-143687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff3c7a7ae68967c3c6de05af768f18d52c2173193bbbccf5672ce56f85ca20e7"} {"question" "q-143687" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/state.json" "file_sha256" "730ea7ef6a323cfca08b62e3d6e93c8cf4e473947b10aed611e83a49ac0aed93"} {"question" "q-143687" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/state.json" "file_sha256" "730ea7ef6a323cfca08b62e3d6e93c8cf4e473947b10aed611e83a49ac0aed93"} {"question" "q-143687" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/state.json" "file_sha256" "730ea7ef6a323cfca08b62e3d6e93c8cf4e473947b10aed611e83a49ac0aed93"} {"question" "q-143688" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $A$ is symmetric, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists).\n- The sum of *any* two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/state.json" "file_sha256" "730ea7ef6a323cfca08b62e3d6e93c8cf4e473947b10aed611e83a49ac0aed93"} {"question" "q-143688" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any non-singular symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{-1})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{-1} \\\\  & ={A}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{T}A)^{T} & ={A}^{T}({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/state.json" "file_sha256" "730ea7ef6a323cfca08b62e3d6e93c8cf4e473947b10aed611e83a49ac0aed93"} {"question" "q-143688" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff3c7a7ae68967c3c6de05af768f18d52c2173193bbbccf5672ce56f85ca20e7"} {"question" "q-143688" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/state.json" "file_sha256" "730ea7ef6a323cfca08b62e3d6e93c8cf4e473947b10aed611e83a49ac0aed93"} {"question" "q-143688" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/state.json" "file_sha256" "730ea7ef6a323cfca08b62e3d6e93c8cf4e473947b10aed611e83a49ac0aed93"} {"question" "q-143688" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070364/state.json" "file_sha256" "730ea7ef6a323cfca08b62e3d6e93c8cf4e473947b10aed611e83a49ac0aed93"}] "usage" {} "basis" 2473 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
